## UI is a return value

A component is a function. It returns what should appear. `App` is that function. There is no separate draw step you call later — the return value is the UI for this render. In this lesson the return is a simple string so you can see the idea without markup noise getting in the way of the rule.

This bites when you `console.log("hello")` inside `App` and return nothing. The log may look like success, but the function's value is `undefined`, so the checked output is wrong. The page, or the harness that prints `App()`, shows what you returned, not what you printed as a side effect.

```javascript
function App() {
  return "hello";
}
console.log(App());
```

A common mistake is returning `"?"` or printing `hello` without returning it. The exercise asks you to make `App` return `hello` so the print of `App()` is exactly that word.
